  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist now. I haven't opened the dashboard to check the panels render, and I haven't seen the rule fire.\n\n**Data:** the logs index has `timestamp`, `response` (the HTTP status, stored as a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host` and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard** \"EFF-ab688aae Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests (a single number)\n- Requests over time (area chart)\n- Responses by status code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar chart)\n\nIt's at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-ab688aae-web-logs-overview-5dc3ee75f7c9`.\n\n**Rule** \"EFF-ab688aae 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and looks at the last 5 minutes of `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It fires when more than 10 requests have a `response.keyword` matching `5xx`. The rule is created disabled by default, so I enabled it. It has no action attached, so it will record alerts but won't notify anyone.",
